{
  "type": "transform_in_place",
  "src": "8-connected yellow marker objects embedded in grey/yellow clipped plus motifs on black background",
  "transform": "for each marker object, find the unique center/up/down/left/right plus containing it and recolor the grey cells of that plus red; preserve black and yellow cells"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 14-19 rows x 15-19 cols
  colors: input uses only black(0), grey(5), yellow(4); output replaces some grey with red(2)
  background: random-looking binary noise of black and grey cells; grey density 40-55% of total grid
  plus_motifs:
    count: 4-7 per grid
    shape: axis-aligned plus (center + up/down/left/right), 5 cells if interior, 3-4 if center on grid edge
    composition: each plus has 2-4 yellow cells and 1-3 grey cells; yellow+grey = plus size
    yellow_connectivity: yellow cells within each plus form one 8-connected component
    uniqueness: each 8-connected yellow component maps to exactly one plus center
    non_overlap: plus motifs share no cells with each other
    separation: manhattan distance between any two plus centers >= 6
    edge_clipping: 0-1 plus centers per grid may sit on grid border (row 0/last or col 0/last) [observed]
    embedding: all plus cells (yellow and grey) sit within the grey+yellow foreground region; no black cell inside any plus
  transform: for each plus, recolor its grey cells to red; yellow and black cells unchanged

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Choose grid size H x W in [14..19] x [15..19].
    2. Generate random binary noise grid of black(0) and grey(5) with grey probability ~45-50%.
    3. Choose N = randint(4, 7) plus motif centers with pairwise manhattan distance >= 6.
       Allow 0-1 centers on the grid edge.
    4. For each center, compute the in-bounds plus cells (3-5 cells).
       Assign each cell randomly to yellow(4) or grey(5), ensuring >=1 grey and >=2 yellow,
       and that the yellow subset is 8-connected. Place these onto the grid (overwriting).
    5. Verify no black cells appear inside any plus (step 2 noise is overwritten in step 4,
       so this is guaranteed by construction).
    6. Build input = the grid from step 4. Build output = copy input, change grey cells
       inside each plus to red(2).
```
